 "estimated_cost_usd": 0.0,
 "summary": "## Security analysis of numpy 2.3.4\n\nI ran CodeQL queries for dangerous sinks, then checked reachability by hand from query locations only. I did not run any proof-of-concept exploits. No high-confidence, remotely exploitable vulnerability turned up. The items below are mostly by-design behaviours or developer-tooling risks.\n\n### Findings\n\n1. **Pickle deserialization in `np.load` and `format.read_array` (CWE-502, medium, documented behaviour).**\n   - Locations: `numpy/lib/_npyio_impl.py:494` (also line 112, `NpzFile` access) and `numpy/lib/_format_impl.py:838`.\n   - `pickle.load` is reached only when `allow_pickle=True`. The default is `False`, and the code raises `ValueError` otherwise (lines 489–492).\n   - It is dangerous only if a caller opts in on untrusted `.npy`/`.npz` files. This is documented and not a library bug.\n\n2. **`eval` on parsed Fortran input in the f2py code generator (CWE-95, low–medium).**\n   - Locations: `numpy/f2py/auxfuncs.py:632, 640, 644`; `capi_maps.py:159, 296, 449`; and many sites in `crackfortran.py` (1329, 2271, 2559, 2637, 2646, 2914, 2985, 3016, 3468).\n   - These evaluate expressions taken from Fortran sources or `.pyf` signature files.\n   - Running f2py on a malicious Fortran or `.pyf` file could therefore execute code. f2py is a developer tool that already compiles and runs code, so the trust boundary is weak.\n\n3. **Template engine `exec`/`eval` (CWE-94, low).**\n   - Locations: `numpy/_build_utils/tempita/_tempita.py:321, 336, 1106`.\n   - This is the vendored Tempita, used at build time on trusted templates. It is not reachable from runtime numpy APIs.\n\n4. **Subprocess use in the f2py meson backend (CWE-78, low).**\n   - Location: `numpy/f2py/_backends/_meson.py:179`.\n   - Command-line arguments from the f2py CLI are passed to `subprocess.run` as a list, not through a shell. This is low risk and only matters if a caller feeds untrusted arguments.\n\n5. **`shell=True` in a developer script (CWE-78, low).**\n   - Location: `numpy/_core/src/npysort/x86-simd-sort/run-bench.py:56, 61, 63`.\n   - This is a benchmark helper that ships in the source tree, not library code.\n\n6. **`numpy.lib._datasource.DataSource` (CWE-22/CWE-918, low).**\n   - Locations: `_datasource.py` lines 328–337 (cache download with `urlopen`, `makedirs`, `copyfileobj`), 411–424 (path building with `join`/`splitdrive`), 475, 584.\n   - This class downloads from user-supplied URLs and writes to a local cache. The code strips the drive and uses `join`, but I did not check path-traversal sanitisation closely.\n   - It is a legacy, little-used API. It is worth a manual review for `..` handling and SSRF if it is exposed to untrusted URLs.\n\n### Other notes\n- The vendored `meson`, `distutils`, `benchmarks`, and `tools` directories contain many `subprocess` and `eval` uses. These are build and test infrastructure and are not part of the runtime attack surface.\n- Several CodeQL queries returned more than 25 results or failed to compile, so coverage of some sink classes is only partial.",
